# Crash Ops OS Architecture Rules

## Read Before Significant Edits

Before making non-trivial changes, read:

- `docs/ARCHITECTURE.md`
- `docs/BUSINESS_RULES.md`
- `docs/DEVELOPMENT_WORKFLOW.md`

For roadmap context, also read `docs/ROADMAP.md`.

## Explain the Plan First

Before editing code, briefly state:

1. The operational problem being solved
2. Which existing engine or service owns the logic
3. Which files you plan to change and why

Do not start editing until the plan is clear.

## Layering Rules

- React components (`src/*.tsx`) display information and collect user input only.
- Business calculations live in reusable modules under `src/engine/` or `src/services/`.
- Never duplicate repair-order normalization, capacity calculations, stage classification, scoring, or recommendation logic in page components.
- Any new engine must remain independent of React (no React imports in `src/engine/` or engine-like services).

## Shared Model and Single Sources of Truth

| Concern | Owner |
|---------|-------|
| Repair order shape | `src/models/RepairOrder.ts` |
| Stage meanings (C/HLD, BOP, PO, HOLD, etc.) | `src/services/stageDictionary.ts` |
| Import normalization | `src/services/importedData.ts` |
| Repair/shop health, risk, priority | `src/engine/operationsEngine.ts` |
| Capacity status and thresholds | `src/engine/capacityEngine.ts` |
| Five-day drop/severity planning | `src/engine/capacityPlanningEngine.ts` |
| Operational recommendations | `src/services/recommendationEngine.ts` |
| Store capacity settings | `src/services/capacitySettings.ts` |

## Data and Navigation

- Imported WIP and capacity settings are stored in browser `localStorage`.
- Navigation is state-driven in `src/App.tsx` — there is no React Router.
- Do not introduce routing libraries unless explicitly requested.

## Quality Gates

After code changes:

1. Run `npm run build` and fix all TypeScript errors.
2. Avoid unused imports and unused declarations.
3. Report any assumptions or uncertain stage/import mappings.
4. Summarize changed files and behavior.

Commit only after the build succeeds and the user has visually validated the change.

## Business Definition Guardrails

Never silently change collision-repair business definitions, including:

- C/HLD (completed repair awaiting pickup/authorization — excluded from active production WIP)
- BOP (back-ordered parts)
- PO (parts ordered)
- Production hold semantics
- Capacity statuses: Capture Keys, Healthy, Flow Delay, True Overload
- Shop capacity setting meanings

If code behavior conflicts with documented rules, flag the conflict instead of redefining the term.

## Refactoring Discipline

- Preserve existing working behavior while refactoring.
- Prefer focused modules and components under roughly 300 lines when practical.
- Do not rewrite large files unless necessary.
- When extracting logic from a component, move it to the appropriate existing engine or service — do not create parallel implementations.

## Intelligence Core Direction

The future Crash Ops Intelligence Core will provide a unified snapshot consumed by all pages. Do not add new per-page recalculation of the same intelligence when a shared snapshot can be used. When building toward Intelligence Core v1, centralize evaluation — do not scatter duplicate engine calls across pages.

## Known Alpha Gaps (Do Not Worsen)

These exist today; document and plan fixes rather than duplicating the pattern:

- Some pages recalculate engines independently instead of consuming a shared snapshot.
- `WipIntelligence` and `WipCapacitySettings` include C/HLD in some WIP hour totals; capacity engines correctly exclude them.
- `cleanNumber` and shop lists are duplicated between `ImportCenter.tsx` and service modules.
- `ProductionBoard.tsx` hardcodes stage column mappings parallel to `stageDictionary.ts`.

When touching these areas, prefer consolidating into the owning service/engine rather than adding a third copy.
